he codimension of the algebraic variety (over the algebraic closure of F) cut out by the gradient. It is known that grk(T) = Θ_d(ark(T)) over any finite field [3, 21], and that grk(T) = Θ_d(prk(T)) over any algebraically closed field [12, 24]. We can now define a geometric analogue to A(d), namely G(d) = max_T prk(T)/grk(T), where the maximum is over all d-linear forms. One can verify that all our results about separating structure and randomness translate, with some effort, to the geometric setting, in which analytic rank is replaced by geometric rank. In particular, G(d) ≥ (log₂(d)+1)/2.\n\n⁵ |F| ≥ (ark(T) + 1)^{ε(d)} would do. It is also known to hold up to a logarithmic factor in ark(T) over any finite field [20].\n\n⁶ One can define a version of A(d) over large enough fields, which is necessarily finite by the aforementioned [5] (even the partition-versus-analytic rank conjecture turned out to be false). The lower bound on A(d) we prove in this paper indeed holds over any finite field.\n\n⁷ The ceiling avoids the rather degenerate case where prk(T) = 1 but the denominator ark(T), which need not be an integer, is close to 0 (when T is a product of many factors).\n\nITCS 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSlice Rank and Partition Rank of the Determinant (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol362-itcs2026/html/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.90/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.90.html)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; ^{6}^{6}6 One can define a version of $A(d)$ over large enough fields, which is necessarily finite by the aforementioned [5] (even if the partition-versus-analytic rank conjecture turned out to be false). ... It seems that separating partition rank from analytic rank – that is, proving the existence of a multilinear form that has high partition rank but low analytic rank – is a nontrivial challenge.\nThis conjecture is known to hold if the field is large enough [5, 20].^{5}^{5}5$|𝔽|\\ge {(\\text{ark}(T)+1)}^{ϵ(d)}$ would do. It is also known to hold up to a logarithmic factor in $\\text{ark}(T)$ over any finite field [20]. The extent to which the bound must depend on $d$, however, is unclear, and the best lower bound we are aware of is a $3/2$ factor [4] (see also [14, 3] for the slightly weaker lower bound of $4/3$ witnessed by the matrix multiplication tensor).\n\nFormally, define the extremal ratio $A(d)={\\mathrm{max}}_{T}\\text{prk}(T)/\\lceil \\text{ark}(T)\\rceil $, where the maximum is over all $d$-linear forms $T$.^{6}^{6}6 One can define a version of $A(d)$ over large enough fields, which is necessarily finite by the aforementioned [5] (even if the partition-versus-analytic rank conjecture turned out to be false). The lower bound on $A(d)$ we prove in this paper indeed holds over any finite field.^{7}^{7}7 The ceiling avoids the rather degenerate case where $\\text{prk}(T)=1$ but the denominator $\\text{ark}(T)$, which need not be an integer, is close to $0$ (when $T$ is a product of many factors). Linear algebra tells us that $A(2)=1$ (that is, $\\text{prk}(T)=\\text{ark}(T)$ for any linear map/bilinear form $T$), and as mentioned above, it is known that $A(3)\\ge \\frac{3}{2}$. (Note also that $A(d)$ is monotone increasing).\n\nIt seems that separating partition rank from analytic rank – that is, proving the existence of a multilinear form that has high partition rank but low analytic rank – is a nontrivial challenge. In particular, for almost all $d$-linear forms $T:{({𝔽}^{n})}^{d}\\to 𝔽$, both $\\text{prk}(T)$ and $\\text{ark}(T)$ are essentially $n$. However, what if we condition $T$ to have a given partition rank? We show that one cannot obtain a separation in this way either.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nopenai/math: 372 claimed theorems, 26 million lines of Lean, and what the green tick means (https://ai.thesatyajit.com/articles/openai-math)\nciteturn7